Q: How do I open a Supademo popup embed from a Next.js App Router page without making the entire page a Client Component? A: <p>Move only the interactive part into a Client Component. The page can remain a Server Component and pass the demo ID as a prop.</p>
<p><code>next/script</code> supports <code>onReady</code>, but script event handlers must be used inside a Client Component. The Supademo popup can then be opened from the button click handler after the SDK has loaded.</p>
<pre><code>// app/features/DemoButton.tsx
'use client';

import { useState } from 'react';
import Script from 'next/script';

declare global {
  interface Window {
    Supademo?: {
      open: (demoId: string) =&gt; void;
    };
  }
}

type DemoButtonProps = {
  demoId: string;
};

export function DemoButton({ demoId }: DemoButtonProps) {
  const [isReady, setIsReady] = useState(false);

  const openDemo = () =&gt; {
    if (!window.Supademo) return;

    window.Supademo.open(demoId);
  };

  return (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;Script
        src=&quot;https://script.supademo.com/supademo.js&quot;
        strategy=&quot;afterInteractive&quot;
        onReady={() =&gt; setIsReady(true)}
      /&gt;

      &lt;button type=&quot;button&quot; onClick={openDemo} disabled={!isReady}&gt;
        {isReady ? 'View walkthrough' : 'Loading walkthrough...'}
      &lt;/button&gt;
    &lt;/&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>
<p>The Server Component only imports and renders the button:</p>
<pre><code>// app/features/page.tsx
import { DemoButton } from './DemoButton';

export default function FeaturesPage() {
  return (
    &lt;main&gt;
      &lt;h1&gt;Feature overview&lt;/h1&gt;

      &lt;DemoButton demoId=&quot;DEMO_ID&quot; /&gt;
    &lt;/main&gt;
  );
}
</code></pre>
<p>This keeps the page itself server-rendered while isolating the browser-only functionality in a small Client Component.</p>
<p>If several routes under the same layout need Supademo buttons, the <code>&lt;Script&gt;</code> component can instead be rendered once in a shared client-side wrapper or provider. Next.js will avoid loading the same layout script again during client-side navigation within that layout.</p>

Q: PHP API routing with parameters A: <pre><code>foreach ($this-&gt;routes as $route =&gt; $params) {
    $pattern = str_replace('{id}', '([0-9]+)', $route);

    // Wrap the pattern in delimiters (#) and add anchors (^ and $) 
    // so it matches the entire url, not just a subset.
    $regex = '#^' . $pattern . '$#';

    if (preg_match($regex, $url['path'], $matches)) {
        $this-&gt;params = $params;

        // Small Bonus: The actual ID matched will be in $matches[1]
        $this-&gt;params['id'] = $matches[1]; 
        break; // Stop looping once we find a match
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: Bitvector verification depends on argument list of function A: <p>You need to specify an ensures clause on lemmas otherwise they won't really do anything useful. Once you have specified an ensures clause and it verifies then you can call them to invoke the pre and postconditions which will allow you to prove more powerful but not entirely obvious facts to the verifier.</p>
<p>You can find more examples <a href="https://dev.to/hath995/dafny-programming-language-and-software-verification-system-2afi" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>module SOBV {
    class Repro {
  var a: bv32

  function myEnc(v: (bv32, bv32), sum: bv32, DUMMY_PLACEHOLDER: bv32): bv32 
  reads this`a
  {
    (((v.1 &lt;&lt; 4) + a))
  }

  lemma myEncLemma_fast(v: (bv32, bv32), sum: bv32) {
    assert 
      myEnc(v, sum, a) ==
      (((v.1 &lt;&lt; 4) + a));
  }

  lemma extraneous(v: (bv32, bv32), sum: bv32)
    ensures forall ph: bv32 :: true ==&gt; myEnc(v, sum, ph) == myEnc(v, sum, 0)
  {
  }

  lemma myEncLemma_slow(v: (bv32, bv32), sum: bv32) {
    extraneous(v, sum);
    assert 
      myEnc(v, sum, 0) ==
      (((v.1 &lt;&lt; 4) + a));
  }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Edit: Alternate</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code> lemma myEncLemma_slow(v: (bv32, bv32), sum: bv32) {
    // extraneous(v, sum);
    calc {
        myEnc(v, sum, 0);
        ((v.1 &lt;&lt; 4) + a);
    }
    assert 
      myEnc(v, sum, 0) ==
      (((v.1 &lt;&lt; 4) + a));
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to convert a flat parent-child list from database into a nested tree structure for JSON response in Spring Boot? A: <p>A clean way to do this is to separate the database model from the response model.</p>
<p>Your repository can return the flat rows, and then your service can build the tree using a <code>Map</code> indexed by <code>id</code>. That avoids searching the parent for every node.</p>
<p>Example DTO for the response:</p>
<pre><code>public class TreeNodeResponse {

    private String id;
    private String value;
    private List&lt;TreeNodeResponse&gt; children = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

    public TreeNodeResponse(String id, String value) {
        this.id = id;
        this.value = value;
    }

    public String getId() {
        return id;
    }

    public String getValue() {
        return value;
    }

    public List&lt;TreeNodeResponse&gt; getChildren() {
        return children;
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>Then in the service you can do something like this:</p>
<pre><code>public List&lt;TreeNodeResponse&gt; buildTree(List&lt;NodeEntity&gt; nodes) {
    Map&lt;String, TreeNodeResponse&gt; index = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
    List&lt;TreeNodeResponse&gt; roots = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();

    for (NodeEntity node : nodes) {
        index.put(
            node.getId(),
            new TreeNodeResponse(node.getId(), node.getName())
        );
    }

    for (NodeEntity node : nodes) {
        TreeNodeResponse current = index.get(node.getId());

        if (node.getParentId() == null) {
            roots.add(current);
        } else {
            TreeNodeResponse parent = index.get(node.getParentId());

            if (parent != null) {
                parent.getChildren().add(current);
            }
        }
    }

    return roots;
}
</code></pre>
<p>The idea is:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>First loop: create all response nodes and store them by <code>id</code>.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Second loop: connect each node to its parent.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Nodes without <code>parentId</code> become roots.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>This is <code>O(n)</code> because each node is processed a constant number of times. It is better than repeatedly searching the parent inside the list, which can become <code>O(n²)</code>.</p>
<p>I would keep this logic in the service layer, not in the controller. The controller should only call the service and return the DTO, then Jackson can serialize the nested structure normally.</p>
<p>One small detail: if your data can contain missing parents or cycles, you may want to validate that separately before returning the tree.</p>
